## Use when

HTTP, CLI, agents or jobs expose the same capability. First locate the current decision owner, persistence owner and real consumers. Extend them before adding another service or transport-specific rule.

Adapters parse and map their protocol. The capability owner applies business policy and coordinates work. Repositories own query/persistence semantics. Select one explicit transaction owner at the existing boundary; helpers use that transaction and do not secretly open sessions or commit. These are responsibilities, not requirements for microservices, singleton objects or an interface for every class.

## Portable example

Save as example.py and run with Python 3.11+. This uses standard-library sqlite3 and json; the [sqlite3 transaction context](https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html#how-to-use-the-connection-context-manager) commits/rolls back, while closing explicitly releases the connection. Two adapter functions share the actual service and database below; they illustrate transport mapping without starting an HTTP server or CLI process. Actors are supplied by the trusted boundary, never by the request body. The fixed actor policy is intentionally small.

~~~python
import json
import sqlite3
from contextlib import closing


class Reports:
    def __init__(self, db):
        self.db = db

    def insert(self, title):
        cursor = self.db.execute("INSERT INTO reports(title) VALUES (?)", (title,))
        return {"id": cursor.lastrowid, "title": title}


class ReportService:
    def __init__(self, db, reports):
        self.db, self.reports = db, reports

    def create(self, actor, title):
        if actor != "owner":
            raise PermissionError()
        if not isinstance(title, str) or not 1 <= len(title.strip()) <= 80:
            raise ValueError()
        with self.db:  # This owner commits on success and rolls back on failure.
            result = self.reports.insert(title.strip())
        return result


def http_create(service, verified_actor, body):
    try:
        if not isinstance(body, dict) or set(body) != {"title"}:
            raise ValueError()
        return 201, {"data": service.create(verified_actor, body["title"])}
    except PermissionError:
        return 403, {"error": "forbidden"}
    except ValueError:
        return 400, {"error": "invalid_input"}
    except sqlite3.DatabaseError:
        return 503, {"error": "unavailable"}


def cli_create(service, verified_actor, args):
    if len(args) != 1:
        return 2, json.dumps({"error": "invalid_input"})
    try:
        return 0, json.dumps({"data": service.create(verified_actor, args[0])})
    except PermissionError:
        return 1, json.dumps({"error": "forbidden"})
    except ValueError:
        return 2, json.dumps({"error": "invalid_input"})
    except sqlite3.DatabaseError:
        return 1, json.dumps({"error": "unavailable"})


def demo():
    with closing(sqlite3.connect(":memory:")) as db:
        db.execute("CREATE TABLE reports(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL)")
        service = ReportService(db, Reports(db))
        assert http_create(service, "owner", {"title": "  Weekly  "}) == (
            201, {"data": {"id": 1, "title": "Weekly"}},
        )
        code, text = cli_create(service, "owner", ["Monthly"])
        assert code == 0 and json.loads(text)["data"] == {"id": 2, "title": "Monthly"}
        assert http_create(service, "foreign", {"title": "Hidden"}) == (
            403, {"error": "forbidden"},
        )
        code, text = cli_create(service, "foreign", ["Hidden"])
        assert code == 1 and json.loads(text) == {"error": "forbidden"}
        assert db.execute("SELECT title FROM reports ORDER BY id").fetchall() == [
            ("Weekly",), ("Monthly",),
        ]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    demo()
~~~

Prove both legitimate and denied calls through each real adapter in the consuming application, including its response envelope and absence of denied mutation. In this example, inject a repository failure after INSERT and assert rollback through a second database read and an unavailable envelope. Adapter-function proof does not establish live routing, authentication or serialization.

## Transactions and external effects

Publish the result after commit, not inside an uncommitted callback. When an existing transaction owner replays callbacks, keep attempts isolated, expire borrowed handles, and keep external sends or other non-replayable effects outside that callback. Use the existing durable-effect/idempotency owner if required; moving a send after commit alone does not make it reliable. Do not introduce retries, outboxes or transaction-port frameworks without a demonstrated need.

Split a growing owner into cohesive collaborators while preserving the decision boundary. Similar syntax in two unrelated domains is not sufficient reason to combine them.

## Observed variants

CFOBooth's NestJS ReportsController and agent report tools invoke the same ReportsService. Its repository database owner uses pg for execution and transaction completion; Kysely compiles queries inside that owner, not through a second executor. Those libraries are conditional on that application. Its driver-double COMMIT-replay test proves the authored lifetime/sequencing expectations, not real PostgreSQL isolation.

StackOS registers a callable contract once. Its MCP and REST dispatchers invoke the registered handler; CLI calls the REST operation route. Those adapters converge on the capability/repository while retaining their protocol duties. Its repository guidance keeps extracted helpers on the caller's Session. That operation registry is StackOS's existing design, not a requirement to build one in every project.

