## Use when

Two real consumers share the meaning of a value set or public shape. Give that contract one owner and derive related variants. Keep implementation-only options, database rows and presentation state near their module. Similar fields do not guarantee identical semantics.

A low-level shared contract should not depend back on applications, database adapters, UI frameworks or test fixtures. Use a small existing leaf module to avoid cycles rather than preserving local mirrors. Central ownership does not mean one giant types package.

## Portable TypeScript example

Place these four files together. TypeScript is required to compile this example; use the consuming project's existing compiler. No runtime schema library is required. See [indexed access types](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/indexed-access-types.html) for Status and [Pick](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/utility-types.html#picktype-keys) for EditorState. Compile with tsc --strict --target ES2022 --module commonjs --outDir out contracts.ts service.ts editor.ts check.ts, then run node out/check.js. The decoder validates an external unknown; compile-time types alone cannot validate network data.

contracts.ts:

~~~typescript
export const STATUSES = ["draft", "ready"] as const;
export type Status = (typeof STATUSES)[number];
export interface Report {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly status: Status;
}

export function decodeStatus(value: unknown): Status {
  for (const status of STATUSES) {
    if (value === status) return status;
  }
  throw new Error("invalid_status");
}
~~~

service.ts:

~~~typescript
import { decodeStatus, type Report } from "./contracts";

export function readReport(id: string, rawStatus: unknown): Report {
  return { id, title: "Report", status: decodeStatus(rawStatus) };
}
~~~

editor.ts:

~~~typescript
import type { Report } from "./contracts";

// Private presentation state; it is not a transport contract.
type EditorState = Pick<Report, "title"> & { readonly dirty: boolean };

export function rename(report: Report, title: string): Report {
  const state: EditorState = { title: title.trim(), dirty: true };
  return { ...report, title: state.title };
}
~~~

check.ts:

~~~typescript
import { decodeStatus, type Status } from "./contracts";
import { rename } from "./editor";
import { readReport } from "./service";

const report = rename(readReport("r-1", "ready"), "  Quarterly  ");
if (report.title !== "Quarterly" || report.status !== "ready") throw new Error("projection");
// @ts-expect-error The canonical contract rejects an unknown status at compile time.
const invalid: Status = "published";
void invalid;
for (const value of ["published", null, {}, 1]) {
  let rejected = false;
  try { decodeStatus(value); } catch { rejected = true; }
  if (!rejected) throw new Error("decoder admitted invalid input");
}
~~~

Change a status at its owner and typecheck the real consumers. Exercise the decoder with independently expected valid/invalid values and test the actual transport envelope separately. Shape validity does not prove actor authority, state transitions or SQL enforcement. Keep semantic mappings explicit where storage and transport deliberately differ.

## Adapt existing tooling

CFOBooth uses a schema-first variant: import its canonical Zod schema, parse at the boundary and derive public TypeScript with z.infer<typeof schema>. Its contracts library excludes server/UI/database runtime dependencies; widget-only state stays in the widget module and refers to shared receipt types.

FlowFilliates uses a type/value-first variant: its framework-free contracts leaf defines constants, tuples, unions and decoders; database and application barrels re-export them. Its Zod validation derives from shared values. Do not impose schema-first direction on that deliberate choice. Its older backend RPC guidance permits local interfaces to avoid a cycle; keep truly private RPC shapes local, but use the newer leaf owner for genuinely shared shapes. Do not turn its wording against “generic interfaces” into a universal ban on useful typed generics.

For an existing cross-language generated path, update the canonical server contract, generation, serializers, fixtures and consumers together. SyncBooth's selected direction is Rust DTO -> OpenAPI -> generated TypeScript; its browser client facade imports the generated client. StackOS's scripts/gen-types.sh generates ui/src/api.ts from an in-process source OpenAPI document. Generation existing in source does not prove generated artifacts are current; run the project's generation/parity checks for an actual contract change. This recommendation neither adds a generator nor certifies those source projects' current generated output.

